Notes on the report of a default ground for practical reasoning

    argue+ for a default (practical) ground whose object is knowledge of whether
      there is a (practical) ground, and (if so) what [it consists in]
        : see @ ../../normative/._/14.default+stable_ground/4_our_practical_binding_to_a_purpose_of_endurance/45.brec
    moral law, conditions that hold
        a) an absolute principle defining the criteria of right action
            : see @ `^^moral law, conditions$`i @ ~/work/ethics/._/text.notes.brec
            - there exists a principle defining the criteria of right action to be one criterion:
              (action) having as an end a practical ground
            - the principle is absolute in being a truth of (practical) logic|reason
            - the right action whose criterion it defines depends on the existence of a practical ground,
              which at present is assured by the existence of a default practical ground
                : ad `at present` : if no other ground exists to be discovered (viz. no stable ground)
                  and we come to learn of this, then the default ground will cease to exist
                  and right action (by this criterion) will be impossible for us
            ∴ this condition holds
        b) its command (addressed demand) by our creator
            : see @ `^^moral law, conditions$`i @ ~/work/ethics/._/text.notes.brec
        c) the creator’s backing of it by force
            : see @ `^^moral law, conditions$`i @ ~/work/ethics/._/text.notes.brec
